DATA=[{'filename': 'como-da-personalidad-a-una-casa-con-pequenos-detalles.html', 'title': 'How to Give a Home Personality With Small Details', 'category': 'HOME DECOR', 'meta': 'Learn how to give your home personality with small changes in color, lighting, textiles, art, plants, personal objects, and thoughtful styling without major renovation.', 'intro': 'A home develops personality through details that reflect the people who live there. Small, intentional changes can make a room feel more personal without replacing the main furniture.', 'body': "\n<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Introduction</li>\n<li>Start With the Atmosphere You Want to Create</li>\n<li>Use Color in Small, Intentional Ways</li>\n<li>Add Texture Through Everyday Objects</li>\n<li>Display Personal Objects Without Creating Clutter</li>\n<li>Improve Lighting to Change the Mood</li>\n<li>Use Art, Books, and Photographs With Intention</li>\n<li>Bring Nature Indoors</li>\n<li>Refresh Textiles and Small Accessories</li>\n<li>Create Focal Points in Ordinary Rooms</li>\n<li>Mix Old and New Pieces</li>\n<li>Give Each Room One or Two Memorable Details</li>\n<li>Avoid Common Decorating Mistakes</li>\n<li>Frequently Asked Questions</li>\n<li>Conclusion</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>A home develops personality through the details that reflect the people who live there. You do not need to replace the furniture, repaint every wall, or follow a strict decorating style. Small changes in color, lighting, textiles, art, plants, books, and meaningful objects can make a room feel more personal without creating unnecessary expense.</p>\n<p>The goal is not to fill every surface. Personality comes from intentional choices and repetition. A few objects with a story, one strong color used in several places, or a consistent mix of materials can make a home feel distinctive while keeping it calm and functional.</p>\n\n<h2>Start With the Atmosphere You Want to Create</h2>\n<p>Before buying accessories, decide how you want each room to feel. A living room may need to feel warm and social, while a bedroom may need to feel calm and restful. A kitchen can feel energetic and practical, and an entryway can feel organized and welcoming.</p>\n<p>This emotional goal helps filter decisions. If you want a room to feel quiet, too many bright colors and decorative objects may work against that intention. If you want a space to feel lively, one or two stronger accents can be more effective than many unrelated items.</p>\n<p>Look at what you already own before purchasing anything. You may already have artwork, books, textiles, ceramics, photographs, or small pieces of furniture that can be moved to another room and used more effectively.</p>\n\n<h2>Use Color in Small, Intentional Ways</h2>\n<p>Color can transform a room without major renovation. Cushion covers, throws, lampshades, small rugs, artwork, and decorative objects can introduce color with relatively little commitment. Choose a limited palette and repeat it in several places to create continuity.</p>\n<p>You do not need to make every element match exactly. A room often feels more natural when different shades of the same color family appear across textiles, objects, and artwork. This creates depth without making the space look overly coordinated.</p>\n<p>If the room already contains strong colors in furniture or flooring, use accessories to support rather than compete with them. Neutral backgrounds can make small colorful details more visible, while darker rooms may benefit from lighter accents that reflect some available light.</p>\n\n<h2>Add Texture Through Everyday Objects</h2>\n<p>Texture adds visual interest even when the color palette is simple. Wood, woven fibers, ceramics, glass, metal, linen-like fabrics, wool, and natural-looking finishes can make a room feel richer without requiring many objects.</p>\n<p>Combine smooth and rough surfaces carefully. A glossy ceramic vase can look more interesting beside a woven basket or textured cloth. A wooden tray can soften a very modern table. The contrast should feel deliberate rather than random.</p>\n<p>Texture is especially useful in neutral interiors. If a room uses mostly beige, white, gray, or earth tones, variation in materials prevents the space from feeling flat.</p>\n\n<h2>Display Personal Objects Without Creating Clutter</h2>\n<p>Photographs, travel souvenirs, handmade pieces, family objects, and inherited items can give a home character because they cannot be reproduced exactly in another house. The key is editing. Display a small number of meaningful objects and give them enough space to be noticed.</p>\n<p>Group related items together instead of spreading them across every surface. Three photographs in similar frames can look more intentional than many unrelated frames placed around the room. A tray can organize small keepsakes and make them easier to clean around.</p>\n<p>Rotate decorative objects periodically. You do not need to keep everything visible at the same time. Changing a few pieces by season or simply when you want a different atmosphere can refresh the room without buying anything new.</p>\n\n<h2>Improve Lighting to Change the Mood</h2>\n<p>Lighting strongly affects how colors, textures, and objects are perceived. A room lit only by one bright ceiling fixture may feel flat even when the decoration is attractive. Adding a floor lamp, table lamp, or wall light can create smaller zones and make the room feel more layered.</p>\n<p>Use lighting according to activity. Reading areas need more focused light, while social areas may feel more comfortable with softer ambient illumination. Decorative lighting should never reduce visibility where practical tasks are performed.</p>\n<p>Pay attention to bulb color temperature and brightness so different fixtures feel related. Excessive contrast between very cool and very warm light can make a room feel visually disconnected.</p>\n\n<h2>Use Art, Books, and Photographs With Intention</h2>\n<p>Artwork can define the personality of a room more quickly than many small accessories. Choose pieces you genuinely enjoy rather than items selected only because they match the sofa. Personal taste is what makes the space feel individual.</p>\n<p>Scale matters. One larger artwork may have more impact than many tiny pieces spread across a large wall. If you prefer a gallery arrangement, plan the spacing before making holes. Paper templates can help you test the composition.</p>\n<p>Books can also be decorative while remaining useful. Keep frequently used books accessible and combine horizontal and vertical arrangements on shelves. Avoid filling every shelf completely so there is some visual breathing room.</p>\n\n<h2>Bring Nature Indoors</h2>\n<p>Plants add color, shape, and life to a room. Choose species according to the actual light, temperature, and care you can provide. A healthy plant in the right place adds more personality than several plants struggling in unsuitable conditions.</p>\n<p>Planters can reinforce the room's style through material and color. Repeating ceramic, terracotta, woven baskets, or simple neutral containers can create consistency even when the plant species differ.</p>\n<p>If the room does not have suitable light for living plants, use other natural elements such as dried branches, wood, stone, or botanical artwork instead of forcing a plant into poor conditions.</p>\n\n<h2>Refresh Textiles and Small Accessories</h2>\n<p>Textiles are one of the easiest ways to change the feeling of a room. Cushion covers, throws, bedding, curtains, and rugs can introduce color and texture without replacing the main furniture.</p>\n<p>Choose materials according to the way the room is used. In homes with children or pets, washable and durable fabrics may be more practical than delicate decorative textiles. Comfort should remain part of the design.</p>\n<p>A small change can be enough. Replacing two cushion covers and moving an existing throw may create more visual improvement than purchasing many unrelated accessories.</p>\n\n<h2>Create Focal Points in Ordinary Rooms</h2>\n<p>A focal point gives the eye a clear place to rest. It can be an artwork, a distinctive lamp, an attractive mirror, a plant, a bookshelf, or a group of objects with strong visual unity.</p>\n<p>Not every wall needs to compete for attention. Supporting areas can remain simpler so the focal point feels deliberate. This approach also helps reduce clutter.</p>\n<p>Use the room's existing architecture when possible. A window, fireplace, niche, or interesting wall can become a focal point with very little additional decoration.</p>\n\n<h2>Mix Old and New Pieces</h2>\n<p>A home often feels more personal when not everything was purchased at the same time. Mixing newer furniture with second-hand, inherited, or vintage objects can create depth and make the space feel collected rather than staged.</p>\n<p>Connection matters more than age. Different pieces can work together when they share a color, material, scale, or visual rhythm. A modern sofa can coexist with an older wooden table if the surrounding elements help relate them.</p>\n<p>Inspect second-hand objects carefully before bringing them home. Check stability, damage, odors, and the amount of repair required. A low purchase price is not useful if restoration becomes unexpectedly expensive.</p>\n\n<h2>Give Each Room One or Two Memorable Details</h2>\n<p>You do not need many decorative statements. One unusual lamp, a large photograph, a distinctive chair, a handmade object, or an interesting color can be enough to give a room identity.</p>\n<p>Choose details that make sense in the context of the household. A beautiful object that interferes with storage, circulation, or cleaning can become frustrating. Personality should support daily life rather than make it more difficult.</p>\n<p>Repeat the same approach throughout the home without repeating the same object. For example, each room can contain one personal focal point and one natural material, creating a shared logic without making every room look identical.</p>\n\n<h2>Avoid Common Decorating Mistakes</h2>\n<p>One common mistake is buying many small objects because the room feels unfinished. This often creates visual noise without solving the real issue. Before buying accessories, check whether the room actually needs better lighting, improved furniture arrangement, or a stronger focal point.</p>\n<p>Another mistake is copying a trend too literally. Trends can provide inspiration, but a home should still reflect the tastes and routines of the people living there. Use trends selectively and combine them with personal elements.</p>\n<p>Finally, avoid covering every wall, shelf, and tabletop. Empty space is part of the composition. It allows meaningful objects to stand out and makes cleaning and organization easier.</p>\n\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>How can I give my home personality on a small budget?</h3>\n<p>Start by rearranging what you already own. Move artwork, books, textiles, and decorative objects between rooms, then add only the small details that are truly missing.</p>\n<h3>Do all rooms need to follow the same style?</h3>\n<p>No. Rooms can have different moods while still feeling connected through repeated colors, materials, or design principles.</p>\n<h3>How many decorative objects should I display?</h3>\n<p>There is no fixed number. Use enough to create interest while leaving visible space around them and keeping surfaces practical.</p>\n<h3>Can second-hand pieces work in a modern home?</h3>\n<p>Yes. Old and new pieces can work together when their proportions, colors, or materials relate to one another.</p>\n\n\n<h2>Use Scent and Sound With Restraint</h2>\n<p>Atmosphere is not only visual. A subtle room fragrance, fresh flowers, or the sound of a small speaker can contribute to a sense of personality. Keep fragrance light, especially in small rooms, because strong scents can become tiring or uncomfortable.</p>\n<p>These sensory details should support the room rather than dominate it. The simplest approach is often the most effective: one pleasant scent, good ventilation, and a calm background are usually enough.</p>\n\n<h2>Use Scent and Sound With Restraint</h2>\n<p>Atmosphere is not only visual. A subtle room fragrance, fresh flowers, or the sound of a small speaker can contribute to a sense of personality. Keep fragrance light, especially in small rooms, because strong scents can become tiring or uncomfortable.</p>\n<p>These sensory details should support the room rather than dominate it. The simplest approach is often the most effective: one pleasant scent, good ventilation, and a calm background are usually enough.</p>\n\n<h2>Use Scent and Sound With Restraint</h2>\n<p>Atmosphere is not only visual. A subtle room fragrance, fresh flowers, or the sound of a small speaker can contribute to a sense of personality. Keep fragrance light, especially in small rooms, because strong scents can become tiring or uncomfortable.</p>\n<p>These sensory details should support the room rather than dominate it. The simplest approach is often the most effective: one pleasant scent, good ventilation, and a calm background are usually enough.</p>\n\n<h2>Use Scent and Sound With Restraint</h2>\n<p>Atmosphere is not only visual. A subtle room fragrance, fresh flowers, or the sound of a small speaker can contribute to a sense of personality. Keep fragrance light, especially in small rooms, because strong scents can become tiring or uncomfortable.</p>\n<p>These sensory details should support the room rather than dominate it. The simplest approach is often the most effective: one pleasant scent, good ventilation, and a calm background are usually enough.</p>\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Giving a home personality does not require major renovation. Small choices in color, texture, lighting, art, plants, textiles, and meaningful objects can make each room feel more individual and welcoming.</p>\n<p>The most effective approach is to edit rather than accumulate. Choose details with a clear purpose, repeat a few visual ideas, and allow personal objects to tell the story of the people who live there. A home feels distinctive when its details are intentional, useful, and genuinely connected to everyday life.</p>\n"}, {'filename': 'como-dar-un-nuevo-aire-al-dormitorio-sin-cambiar-los-muebles.html', 'title': 'How to Refresh a Bedroom Without Replacing the Furniture', 'category': 'HOME DECOR', 'meta': 'Learn how to refresh a bedroom without replacing the furniture by changing the layout, bedding, lighting, curtains, wall treatment, organization, and small decorative details.', 'intro': 'A bedroom can feel completely different without replacing the main furniture. Layout, textiles, lighting, organization, and a few visual changes can transform the space at relatively low cost.', 'body': "\n<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Introduction</li>\n<li>Rearrange the Furniture Before Buying Anything</li>\n<li>Refresh the Bed With Textiles</li>\n<li>Improve Lighting</li>\n<li>Change the Wall Without Replacing Furniture</li>\n<li>Use Curtains and Rugs to Transform the Atmosphere</li>\n<li>Declutter and Improve Storage</li>\n<li>Add Plants and Natural Elements</li>\n<li>Refresh Small Furniture Details</li>\n<li>Create a Stronger Focal Point</li>\n<li>Improve the Bedroom Through Better Styling</li>\n<li>Make Seasonal Changes Without Overspending</li>\n<li>Avoid Common Bedroom Refresh Mistakes</li>\n<li>Frequently Asked Questions</li>\n<li>Conclusion</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>A bedroom can feel completely different without replacing the main furniture. Changing the layout, bedding, lighting, curtains, wall treatment, accessories, and organization can refresh the space while keeping the bed, wardrobe, dresser, and other major pieces you already own.</p>\n<p>The best place to begin is by identifying what currently makes the room feel tired. It may be poor lighting, too much clutter, an awkward layout, worn textiles, or a lack of visual focus. Solving the real problem usually creates more impact than purchasing random decorative items.</p>\n\n<h2>Rearrange the Furniture Before Buying Anything</h2>\n<p>Furniture placement changes how the room feels and how easily it functions. If possible, move the bed to a wall where it feels more anchored and where circulation around it is comfortable. Check door swing, wardrobe access, windows, and walking routes before making the final decision.</p>\n<p>Do not assume the original layout is the only option. Moving a dresser to another wall or changing the position of bedside tables can create more visual balance without any cost.</p>\n<p>Use painter's tape to test larger changes before moving heavy furniture. Mark the new footprint on the floor and check whether drawers, doors, and windows can still open fully.</p>\n<p>When the room is small, remove one unnecessary piece before adding anything new. More open floor area can make a bigger difference than another decorative table or chair.</p>\n\n<h2>Refresh the Bed With Textiles</h2>\n<p>The bed is usually the largest visual element in the bedroom, so changing bedding can transform the room quickly. A new duvet cover, bedspread, throw, or pair of cushion covers can introduce a new color palette without replacing the furniture.</p>\n<p>Layering creates depth. Combine smooth bedding with one textured blanket or a small number of cushions. Too many pillows can make the bed look elaborate but create extra work every day.</p>\n<p>Choose materials according to climate and comfort. Breathable fabrics may be preferable in warm conditions, while heavier textures can make the room feel cozier during cooler months.</p>\n<p>Repeat one or two colors from the bedding elsewhere in the room through artwork, curtains, or small accessories. This creates cohesion without requiring an identical matching set.</p>\n\n<h2>Improve Lighting</h2>\n<p>Lighting can make a familiar bedroom feel new. Instead of relying only on a ceiling fixture, add or reposition bedside lamps, a floor lamp, or wall lighting where appropriate. Layered light creates more flexibility for reading, dressing, and relaxing.</p>\n<p>Choose bedside lighting that is easy to reach from the bed. If two people use the room, balanced lighting on both sides can improve symmetry and function.</p>\n<p>Bulb brightness and color temperature influence atmosphere. Very harsh light can make the bedroom feel less restful, while extremely dim light may be impractical. Combine comfortable ambient lighting with brighter task lighting where needed.</p>\n<p>Manage cables carefully. Lamps and chargers should not create trip hazards or clutter around the bed. A simple cable clip or hidden routing can improve the appearance of a bedside area.</p>\n\n<h2>Change the Wall Without Replacing Furniture</h2>\n<p>Paint is one of the most effective ways to refresh a bedroom. Repainting all walls can create a major change, but an accent wall may be enough if the rest of the room is in good condition.</p>\n<p>Before choosing a color, look at the existing furniture and flooring. A new wall color should work with the undertones already present. Test samples in several parts of the room because natural and artificial light can change their appearance.</p>\n<p>If painting is not possible, large artwork, removable wall treatments, or a well-arranged gallery can change the visual focus. Keep the scale appropriate to the bed and wall rather than using many small pieces that feel disconnected.</p>\n<p>The wall behind the headboard is a natural place for a focal point, but it is not mandatory. A window, artwork, or attractive opposite wall can also become the strongest visual feature.</p>\n\n<h2>Use Curtains and Rugs to Transform the Atmosphere</h2>\n<p>Curtains affect both light and the visual height of the room. Hanging them higher and allowing them to frame the window can make the wall feel taller, provided the rod and installation are suitable.</p>\n<p>Choose a curtain length that works with the floor and cleaning routine. Fabrics should support privacy and light control without creating unnecessary bulk.</p>\n<p>A rug can add softness and visually connect the bed to the rest of the room. Measure carefully. A rug that is too small may look disconnected, while one that is too large can interfere with doors or make cleaning difficult.</p>\n<p>If the room already has carpet or a strong floor pattern, another rug may not be necessary. Evaluate whether the addition solves a real visual or comfort need.</p>\n\n<h2>Declutter and Improve Storage</h2>\n<p>A bedroom often looks tired because too many objects remain visible. Clear bedside tables, dresser tops, and the floor before adding decoration. Removing clutter can immediately make the same furniture look more intentional.</p>\n<p>Store items close to where they are used. Keep nighttime essentials in a bedside drawer, clothing in the wardrobe, and occasional accessories in closed storage. Avoid using chairs as permanent clothing storage.</p>\n<p>Under-bed storage can be useful when space is limited, but it should remain accessible and organized. Containers with lids can reduce dust and keep seasonal items separate.</p>\n<p>Review the wardrobe as well. Removing unused clothing often creates enough space to organize the remaining items more effectively without buying new storage furniture.</p>\n\n<h2>Add Plants and Natural Elements</h2>\n<p>A suitable plant can add freshness and color to the bedroom. Choose one according to the actual light and the amount of care you can provide. Do not place plants in dark corners simply because they look empty.</p>\n<p>Wood, stone, woven fibers, linen-like textiles, or simple ceramics can also introduce a natural feeling without living plants. These materials can soften a room dominated by manufactured surfaces.</p>\n<p>Keep plant pots away from bedding and furniture where moisture could cause damage. Use stable containers and protective saucers when necessary.</p>\n\n<h2>Refresh Small Furniture Details</h2>\n<p>You can change the appearance of existing furniture through small, reversible details. Replacing drawer handles, cleaning and polishing suitable surfaces, or adding a tray to a dresser can make familiar pieces feel more refined.</p>\n<p>Do not repaint or refinish furniture impulsively. First identify the material and finish, and decide whether the intervention is appropriate. Some pieces have coatings or veneers that require special preparation.</p>\n<p>Before purchasing new hardware, measure the existing holes so replacements fit without unnecessary drilling. Keep the original hardware in case you want to restore the previous look later.</p>\n\n<h2>Create a Stronger Focal Point</h2>\n<p>The bedroom usually benefits from one clear focal point. The bed itself can become that focal point through bedding, artwork, lighting, or a headboard treatment. This helps the room feel more organized.</p>\n<p>Avoid making every wall compete for attention. If the bed wall is visually strong, the dresser wall can remain simpler. This creates contrast and keeps the room calm.</p>\n<p>Scale the focal point appropriately. One large artwork can have more impact than many small pieces. A strong but simple visual idea often looks more sophisticated than a collection of unrelated decorations.</p>\n\n<h2>Improve the Bedroom Through Better Styling</h2>\n<p>Styling is about arrangement, not only purchasing. Group objects on a dresser instead of spreading them evenly. Use a tray for perfume or small accessories. Leave some visible empty surface so the furniture remains functional.</p>\n<p>Bedside tables should hold only what is genuinely useful at night. A lamp, book, water, and one small decorative item may be enough.</p>\n<p>Repeat materials or colors in several places. If the room includes black metal lamps, one black frame elsewhere can create visual connection. If the furniture is warm wood, textiles with warm undertones can help the room feel cohesive.</p>\n\n<h2>Make Seasonal Changes Without Overspending</h2>\n<p>Small seasonal updates can keep the room feeling fresh. Change a throw, cushion cover, or small piece of art rather than replacing the entire bedding set.</p>\n<p>Store off-season textiles properly and rotate them when needed. This can extend their useful life and reduce unnecessary purchases.</p>\n<p>Natural light also changes through the year. Review curtain position and lighting so the bedroom remains comfortable in both brighter and darker months.</p>\n\n<h2>Avoid Common Bedroom Refresh Mistakes</h2>\n<p>One common mistake is buying decoration before resolving clutter and layout. A room filled with new accessories can still feel disorganized if circulation and storage are poor.</p>\n<p>Another mistake is choosing a new color without considering existing furniture and flooring. Testing samples first reduces the chance of creating an undertone conflict.</p>\n<p>Avoid overcrowding the bed with cushions and throws that are removed every night and placed on the floor. Decorative choices should remain manageable.</p>\n<p>Finally, do not copy a trend so completely that the room loses personal character. Use trends as inspiration while keeping objects, colors, and materials that genuinely suit your preferences.</p>\n\n<h2>Review the Bedroom After the Changes</h2>\n<p>Live with the refreshed room for at least a week before deciding that more is needed. Notice whether lighting is practical, whether the new arrangement improves circulation, and whether surfaces remain easy to keep clear.</p>\n<p>If something still feels wrong, identify the specific issue. The solution may be moving a lamp, removing one object, or adjusting a curtain rather than buying more furniture.</p>\n<p>Photographs can help you see the room objectively. A quick photo often reveals clutter, scale problems, or empty areas differently from standing inside the space every day.</p>\n\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>How can I refresh a bedroom without buying new furniture?</h3>\n<p>Change the layout, bedding, lighting, curtains, wall color or artwork, organization, and small accessories. These elements can transform the room while keeping the main furniture.</p>\n<h3>What gives the biggest visual impact?</h3>\n<p>The bed, wall color, and lighting usually have the strongest effect because they occupy or influence large visual areas.</p>\n<h3>Should I repaint the furniture?</h3>\n<p>Only if the material and finish are suitable and the change is worth the work. Many rooms can be refreshed without altering the furniture itself.</p>\n<h3>How can I make a small bedroom feel larger?</h3>\n<p>Reduce clutter, preserve visible floor area, use correctly scaled furniture, improve light, and avoid filling every wall and surface with decoration.</p>\n\n\n<h2>Refresh the Room With Existing Objects</h2>\n<p>Before shopping, collect small items from other rooms and test them in the bedroom. A lamp from the living room, a mirror from the hallway, or artwork stored away may work better in the refreshed layout. This approach reduces spending and helps you see familiar objects differently.</p>\n<p>Change only a few items at a time so the room does not lose coherence. Once the main layout, bedding, and lighting feel right, smaller objects can be added selectively.</p>\n\n<h2>Refresh the Room With Existing Objects</h2>\n<p>Before shopping, collect small items from other rooms and test them in the bedroom. A lamp from the living room, a mirror from the hallway, or artwork stored away may work better in the refreshed layout. This approach reduces spending and helps you see familiar objects differently.</p>\n<p>Change only a few items at a time so the room does not lose coherence. Once the main layout, bedding, and lighting feel right, smaller objects can be added selectively.</p>\n\n<h2>Refresh the Room With Existing Objects</h2>\n<p>Before shopping, collect small items from other rooms and test them in the bedroom. A lamp from the living room, a mirror from the hallway, or artwork stored away may work better in the refreshed layout. This approach reduces spending and helps you see familiar objects differently.</p>\n<p>Change only a few items at a time so the room does not lose coherence. Once the main layout, bedding, and lighting feel right, smaller objects can be added selectively.</p>\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>A bedroom can feel completely renewed without replacing the main furniture. Reorganizing the layout, refreshing textiles, improving lighting, simplifying storage, and changing a few visual details can produce a major difference at a relatively low cost.</p>\n<p>Start with the changes that solve real problems, then add color, art, plants, and accessories gradually. A successful refresh makes the room more comfortable, organized, and personal while preserving the furniture that already works well.</p>\n"}]

def wc(h):
    return len(re.findall(r"\b[\w'-]+\b",re.sub(r"<[^>]+>"," ",h)))

print("="*68)
print("NEXT BATCH - ARTICLES 35 AND 36")
print("="*68)

for d in DATA:
    p=ART/d["filename"]
    if not p.exists():
        print("ERROR - file not found:",d["filename"])
        raise SystemExit(1)
    n=wc(d["body"])
    if not 2000 <= n <= 3000:
        print("ERROR - invalid word count:",d["filename"],n)
        raise SystemExit(1)

for d in DATA:
    p=ART/d["filename"]
    t=p.read_text(encoding="utf-8",errors="replace")
    b=BACK/d["filename"]
    if not b.exists():
        shutil.copy2(p,b)

    t=re.sub(r'<html\s+lang=["\']es["\']','<html lang="en"',t,flags=re.I)
    t=re.sub(r'(<h1[^>]*>).*?(</h1>)',lambda m:m.group(1)+d["title"]+m.group(2),t,count=1,flags=re.I|re.S)
    t=re.sub(r'(<title[^>]*>).*?(</title>)',lambda m:m.group(1)+d["title"]+" | Home & Garden"+m.group(2),t,count=1,flags=re.I|re.S)
    t=re.sub(r'(<meta[^>]+name=["\']description["\'][^>]+content=["\'])[^"\']*(["\'])',lambda m:m.group(1)+d["meta"]+m.group(2),t,count=1,flags=re.I|re.S)
    t=re.sub(r'(<p[^>]+class=["\'][^"\']*article-intro[^"\']*["\'][^>]*>).*?(</p>)',lambda m:m.group(1)+d["intro"]+m.group(2),t,count=1,flags=re.I|re.S)
    t=re.sub(r'(<span[^>]+class=["\'][^"\']*article-category[^"\']*["\'][^>]*>).*?(</span>)',lambda m:m.group(1)+d["category"]+m.group(2),t,count=1,flags=re.I|re.S)

    pat=r'(<article\b[^>]*class=["\'][^"\']*article-content-full[^"\']*["\'][^>]*>).*?(</article>)'
    t,n=re.subn(pat,lambda m:m.group(1)+"\n"+d["body"]+"\n"+m.group(2),t,count=1,flags=re.I|re.S)
    if n!=1:
        print("ERROR - article-content-full not found:",d["filename"])
        raise SystemExit(1)

    for old,new in {
        "Casa & Jardín":"Home & Garden",
        "Equipo Editorial de Casa & Jardín":"Home & Garden Editorial Team",
        "Publicado:":"Published:",
        "Actualizado:":"Updated:",
        "Inicio":"Home",
        "Categorías":"Categories",
        "Sobre nosotros":"About Us",
        "Contacto":"Contact",
        "Compartir":"Share",
        "También puede interesarte":"You May Also Like",
        "También te puede interesar":"You May Also Like",
        "DECORACIÓN":"HOME DECOR"
    }.items():
        t=t.replace(old,new)

    p.write_text(t,encoding="utf-8")
    print(f'OK - {d["filename"]} -> {wc(d["body"])} words')

